WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.800
Netflix's deliciously dark adaptation of the
best-selling books A Series of Unfortunate

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.330
Events is absolutely loaded with easter eggs
and references.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.860
Yippee ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video I'm going to whip through

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.630
some of my favourite easter eggs, cameos,
and connections to TV shows, movies, and Lemony

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.610
Snicket's original books that I found in the
TV series.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:26.660
Ok, quick warning: there are spoilers ahead,
so if you want to avoid those, look away,

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:27.660
look away!

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.650
But don't forget to come back here when you're
ready!

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:34.460
Before this show, actress Malina Weissman,
who plays Violet Baudelaire, was best known

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.670
for her role as the young Kara Zor-El in the
TV show Supergirl, and there's a great Supergirl

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:41.980
hat-tip in Part One of The Reptile Room.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.970
Yes, when Violet and her siblings sit down
to eat with their guardian Dr Monty Montgomery

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:50.909
and Stephano, Violet asks, 'Could you please
pass the pot stickers?'

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:55.140
Which is a delicious reference to Supergirl's
love of that particular food, which we've

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.989
seen a number of times in the Supergirl TV
series!

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:03.449
If only Violet could have 'melted' Count Olaf's
face when he deliberately ate all the pot

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:08.180
stickers, the way that Kara jokingly told
her sister she'd do in Supergirl, then the

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.079
Baudelaire children wouldn't have had to deal
with all those unfortunate events!

00:01:12.079 --> 00:01:16.829
During the tunnel scenes in the first episode,
Lemony Snicket walks past a variety of signs,

00:01:16.829 --> 00:01:20.520
many of which are references to characters
in the original books, some of whom we'll

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.510
no doubt see in future seasons.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.829
For example, there's a sign for JULIENNE,
which is a shout-out to Geraldine Julienne,

00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:31.430
a reporter at The Daily Punctilio, where Eleanora
Poe is the Editor-in-Chief.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:35.960
Speaking of which, although Julienne hasn't
appeared in the TV series yet, Eleanora Poe

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.250
does mention her 'star reporter' who wrote
a front-page feature about the Baudelaire

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:45.869
Mansion fire, and whose by-line we see on
another article about the Baudelaire children.

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:50.160
Another sign we see is for REMORA, which is
a reference to Mr Remora, a teacher at the

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.939
Prep School where Violet, Klaus and Sunny
find themselves at the end of season one.

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:59.380
And that's also where, in the book The Austere
Academy, the Baudelaires meet the very mean

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.150
schoolgirl Carmelita SPATS.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:06.280
As for the sign for WIDDERSHINS, that refers
to Captain Widdershins, the step-father of

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.500
the Hook-Handed Man, who also gets a sign
in the tunnels featuring his name FERNALD.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.690
There are also signs for characters who appear
in later episodes of season one, including

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:20.050
Montgomery, the children's guardian; Anwhistle,
the surname of the Baudelaire's guardian,

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:26.690
Aunt Josephine; Strauss, Count Olaf's neighbour
Justice Strauss; Snicket, a reference to writer-narrator

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:31.299
Lemony or his siblings Jacques and Kit; and
Quagmire, the surname of Cobie Smulders and

00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:35.260
Will Arnett's mother and father characters
whose children end up at the same school as

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:36.260
the Baudelaires.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.569
Over the course of season one of the show,
there are a number of nods to All The Wrong

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:44.010
Questions, a collection of four books that
serve as a prequel to the Series of Unfortunate

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.010
Events novels.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:48.610
In episode 3, Klaus asks Violet, 'How did
he find us?'

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.940
when Count Olaf turns up disguised as Stephano
at Uncle Monty's house, to which Violet replies,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.940
'That's the wrong question.'

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:00.240
And in episode 7, after the children discuss
whether Count Olaf will find them at the mill,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.090
narrator Lemony Snicket says, 'Violet was
asking the wrong questions.'

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:08.560
Also, in episode 2, when Violet and Klaus
are looking for their missing sister, Sunny,

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.430
the dastardly Count asks them, 'When did you
see her last?', which is actually the title

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.400
of the second book in the All The Wrong Questions
series.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.720
In real life, Lemony Snicket is actually the
pen name for author Daniel Handler.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.000
And, although actor Patrick Warburton plays
the part of Snicket on screen, Handler does

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.629
actually appear three times in the show as
well!

00:03:27.629 --> 00:03:32.020
In episode 5, when Aunt Josephine is shopping
at the market, Handler pops up selling fish

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.709
heads at a street stall!

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:38.230
And in episode 6, as a storm's coming, he
runs across the street yelling 'save the fish

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.230
heads!'

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:43.550
Then in episode 7, he makes another appearance,
this time interacting with Mr Poe, who's desperate

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:47.900
to find the Baudelaire children, who he doesn't
realise have run away to the Lucky Smells

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:48.900
Lumbermill.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.760
Mr Poe looks at the camera and cries 'It's
a catastrophe!

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:52.939
It's unprecedented!

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:53.939
It's off-book!

00:03:53.939 --> 00:03:58.530
It's unfortunate!', then he turns to Handler's
fish-head seller and says, 'The Baudelaires

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.400
are lost and unsupervised!

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.640
Do you know what that means?', to which Handler
replies, 'Without supervision.'

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.840
This is an ingeniously meta moment as, in
the original books, Mr Poe knows exactly where

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.420
the children are because he takes them to
Paltreyville where the Mill is to meet their

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:19.490
next guardian, so that part of the story in
the TV series has quite literally gone 'off-book'!

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:24.979
In Part Two of The Wide Window, after Count
Olaf has forced Josephine to write a will

00:04:24.979 --> 00:04:30.060
leaving the children in his care, she rushes
into her library to try to figure out a means

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.060
of escape.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:35.300
And when Olaf, who's disguised as Captain
Sham at the time, notices that Josephine has

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:40.610
shut the library doors behind her, he hobbles
up the hallway after her, saying 'Here's Shammy!'

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:44.900
Which is an homage to Stanley Kubrick's movie
The Shining and Jack Nicholson's famous line,

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:49.150
'Here's Johnny', which he says as he breaks
down the door to the bathroom where his character's

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.729
wife is hiding.

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:55.540
In 2004, the first three books in Lemony Snicket's
A Series of Unfortunate Events were adapted

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:58.060
into a movie starring Jim Carrey as Count
Olaf.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:02.470
And, like Netflix's version of the Baudelaires'
story, that movie featured the character of

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:07.139
Justice Strauss, who was played by an actress
called Catherine O'Hara, whose name should

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:10.639
ring a bell if you've watched the closing
credits of A Series of Unfortunate Events

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:16.740
on Netflix as this time Catherine O'Hara plays
the scheming optometrist Dr Georgina Orwell

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:17.740
in the show!

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:21.940
Which adds another meta layer of humour to
the scene where Dr Orwell tells Klaus about

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:27.150
an optometrist who changed her looks 'to assume
a new identity in a faraway town'.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:31.170
In the season finale, there's a lovely easter
egg to the books as well as a tease for season

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:36.580
two when our narrator Lemony Snicket explains
hypnosis to us, saying that, 'The 'Encyclopedia

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:41.060
Hypnotica tells of […] a man who, upon hearing
the word "Nero," could suddenly play an instrument

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:42.319
he'd never studied.'

00:05:42.319 --> 00:05:46.139
Then the camera cuts to a woman playing a
violin in a movie that Snicket's watching

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:47.150
on TV.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.950
This is a nice nod to the character of Vice
Principal Nero, who runs the boarding school

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:54.070
where we see the Baudelaire children at the
end of season one.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:59.360
In the books, Vice Principal Nero is a nasty
character and an awful violin player who forces

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.790
students to listen to his performances.

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:06.110
His name and violin obsession were likely
inspired by the Roman Emperor Nero and the

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:07.110
expression 'Nero fiddled while Rome burned.'

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:10.750
And, towards the end of the eighth episode,
we also see the silhouette of a person playing

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:15.470
the violin, again, letting us know that we'll
be seeing Vice Principal Nero in season two.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:19.410
Of course, there are tons more easter eggs
and references in the show, but what were

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:20.410
your favourites?

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:23.199
And what are you looking forward to seeing
in season 2 and who would you like to see

00:06:23.199 --> 00:06:24.550
cast in some of the new roles?

